      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hi, I'm looking for some clarification on UK inheritance tax (IHT) regarding assets held in offshore trusts. If a deceased individual was a UK resident but held significant real estate in a non-resident trust, how is the valuation of those specific assets treated for the purpose of calculating the taxable estate? Also, are there any common pitfalls where assets might be overlooked during the initial filing?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Inheritance Tax (IHT) in the UK can be a complex and nuanced topic, but I'll try to break it down for you. When it comes to assets held in offshore trusts, the UK's IHT rules can be quite tricky, but I'll try to provide some clarity. \n\nFor assets held in a non-resident trust, the UK's IHT rules require the trust to be valued at the \"market value\" of the assets at the date of the deceased person's death. This is often referred to as the \"market value\" or \"valuation date\" for IHT purposes. It's worth noting that the trust's accounting date may not necessarily be the same as the valuation date, so it's essential to ensure that the correct date is used for IHT purposes.\n\nNow, when it comes to valuing the assets in the trust, the UK's IHT rules require that the valuation be done by a qualified valuer, such as a chartered surveyor or a qualified accountant. The valuer must use a suitable valuation method, such as the \"income approach\" or the \"capital values approach,\" to determine the market value of the assets.\n\nAs for common pitfalls, one area to watch out for is ensuring",
      "source": "target",
